sec.p:
Geometries of all minimum energy structures, positive vibrational frequencies, and saddle points, negative eigenvalue in Hessian matrix for imaginary frequency, were optimized at the B3LYP level of theory78,79 with the 6-31G(d,p) basis sets using Gaussian09 quantum chemistry package80. The solvent was modeled by polarized continuum model (PCM) employing the solvation model based on density (SMD) in the calculations81,82. Rate constants were calculated by the transition state theory (TST)83 and corrected by Wigner approximation84,85 (for more details see Supplementary Data). To verify the reliability of the B3LYP/6-31G(d,p) theory for TS geometries, we re-optimized transition states (TSs) in both gas and ethanol phase by a another level of computational method M06-2X86 at the same basis set and solvent model as recommended by Linder and Brinck study87. The frontier orbitals of anti and syn molecules were analyzed at the same level of theory using the natural bond orbital (NBO) approach introduced by Weinhold et al.88,89. Also, hydrogen chemical shifts of anti and syn molecules are calculated on the optimized geometries with the gauge-invariant atomic orbital (GIAO) approach at the same level of theory with tetramethylsilane, TMS, as a reference at the B3LYP with 6-311+G(2d,p) basis set while the hydrogen chemical shifts are the difference of these two numbers90,91. The aromaticity index, nuclear independent chemical shifts (NICS)92,93, which is the negative of isotropic magnetic shielding, has been calculated at the ring center and 1 Å above the ring center in both mean principal axes and along the \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
